C/ Isolates 239-STARTUP.hal's `SCHEDULE ... ON (ORBIT | (ORBIT2 | ORBIT3))`
C/ construct (a compound BOR event-expression) -- proves WORKER starts as
C/ soon as ANY ONE of the three events becomes true, not needing all of
C/ them (the OR-vs-AND counterpart of test_sched_on_compound.hal).
C/
C/ Scheduling is non-pre-emptive (USA003087 Sec. 13.1), so the WAIT after
C/ the SIGNAL is what yields to the scheduler and lets the now-armed WORKER
C/ dispatch -- as the real 239-STARTUP context provides a yield.  A single
C/ SIGNAL satisfies the OR, so WORKER runs during that WAIT, before the
C/ following WRITE.

  TSCHDOR: PROGRAM;
     DECLARE E1 EVENT LATCHED, E2 EVENT LATCHED, E3 EVENT LATCHED;

  WORKER:
  TASK;
     WRITE(6) 'WORKER STARTED';
  CLOSE WORKER;

     SCHEDULE WORKER ON (E1 | (E2 | E3)) PRIORITY(70);
     WRITE(6) 'BEFORE ANY SIGNAL';
     SIGNAL E2;
     WAIT(5);
     WRITE(6) 'AFTER E2';
  CLOSE TSCHDOR;
